hub.com/users/happybeing/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2020-10-08T13:58:11Z","updated_at":"2020-10-08T13:58:11Z","body":"I didn't have `npm` installed and had skipped the following because I already had set up `node` with `nvm`. When I inspected the line where the error is I saw it using `npm`. The following has fixed this issue:\r\n```\r\nnvm install node --latest-npm\r\nnvm use node\r\n```\r\n\r\nI think this is an edge case few will hit, but given the Tauri team ecourage people to use `yarn`, someone like myself who already prefers `yarn` may well not have `npm` installed, particularly on a recently set-up machine. That was my issue.\r\n\r\nSo maybe tauri init should check for existance of programs it uses such as `npm`? I'll leave this open to register that question, but feel free to close.","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/tauri-apps/tauri/issues/comments/705589015/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":null,"event":"commented","actor":{"login":"happybeing","id":2183895,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjIxODM4OTU=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/2183895?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing","html_url":"https://github.com/happybeing","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/happybeing/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https:/